# openclaw EGRESS GATEWAY — v4.1 Phase 1c (LAURA-I1-03 close, compose side)
# Last updated: 2026-07-06
#
# SINGLE SOURCE — CANONICAL FILE (v4.1 unified-sidecar must-fix #9, Captain C5).
# This file is imported (top-level) by every Caddyfile variant
#   docker/Caddyfile.{selfsigned,ca,acme}   via  import /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.openclaw-egress
# and mirrored BYTE-IDENTICAL into the Helm chart at
#   helm/yashigani/files/Caddyfile.openclaw-egress
# by scripts/sync-caddyfile-egress-helm.sh (same pattern as the
# mcp.rego → helm/yashigani/files/policy/ fix).  NEVER edit the helm copy —
# edit THIS file, then run:  scripts/sync-caddyfile-egress-helm.sh
# Drift gate: tests/contracts/test_openclaw_egress_single_source.py fails on
# any byte difference AND on any re-inlined hand-coded copy in configmaps.yaml.
#
# K8s render: helm/yashigani/templates/configmaps.yaml loads the mirror via
# .Files.Get and applies EXACTLY these documented template substitutions
# (the only compose↔K8s deltas; anything else is drift):
#   1. https://gateway:8080            → https://yashigani-gateway:8080
#   2. /run/secrets/ca_intermediate.crt → /run/secrets/ca_bundle.crt
#   3. spiffe://yashigani.internal/     → spiffe://<.Values.global.spiffeTrustDomain>/
#   4. {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID:} default ← .Values.agentBundles.openclaw.telegramBotId
# plus a K8s-side (internal-mtls) snippet shim (compose defines the snippet in
# the Caddyfile.{selfsigned,ca,acme} monoliths; K8s defines it next to the
# .Files.Get include with the ca_bundle.crt trust anchor).
#
# WHY (LAURA-I1-03, High/Bypass, proven live): openclaw was dual-homed on the
# `edge` bridge (internal:false) with arbitrary internet egress while holding
# YASHIGANI_INTERNAL_BEARER — a one-hop exfiltration path. Fix: openclaw leaves
# `edge` entirely; its ONLY outbound path to the internet is THIS gateway.
#
# DESIGN (Laura threat model laura-forwardproxy-threatmodel.md, GO-WITH-FIXES):
#   FP-01 — this is a FIXED-UPSTREAM reverse_proxy gateway, NOT a CONNECT/open
#           forward proxy. The destination set is the three literal upstreams
#           below. openclaw never supplies a destination host — the entire
#           open-relay / SSRF / DNS-rebind / IP-literal / domain-fronting class
#           has no input field to attack. caddyserver/forwardproxy is
#           deliberately NOT used (NO-GO condition).
#   FP-02 — the allowlist IS this file (perimeter-owned, single-source render).
#           No destination host exists in openclaw.json / openclaw env — a
#           compromised openclaw cannot rewrite its own destination set.
#           Enforced by tests/invariants/test_openclaw_egress_gateway.py.
#   FP-03 — caller auth = openclaw's per-instance mesh leaf. The listener
#           requires_and_verifies a leaf from the internal intermediate CA AND
#           each route hard-matches the URI SAN against openclaw's SPIFFE ID
#           (parse-time env {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID}; compose composes it
#           from the per-instance trust domain). Any other mesh peer — even one
#           holding a valid leaf (e.g. gateway) — is refused 403. The listener
#           additionally rides a dedicated 2-member bridge (openclaw_ringfence:
#           openclaw + caddy ONLY, internal:true) so no other container can even
#           dial :18790. NOT bearer-auth (FP-08: the baked bearer is exactly
#           what an openclaw attacker holds).
#   FP-04 — moot by construction: Caddy resolves the fixed hosts itself, dials
#           :443 only, verifies the upstream cert against the system (public)
#           CA pool [transport tls default], and pins SNI via tls_server_name.
#   FP-07 — hooks.slack.com (Slack response_url callbacks) and api.telegram.org
#           (bot API + /file/bot<token>/... downloads) are IN the allowlist as
#           exact FQDNs (no wildcards), so those legitimate callbacks flow
#           through THIS gateway — no second unfiltered egress path exists
#           (openclaw has no other route to the internet at L3).
#   FP-06 — Phase 2 CLOSED (this commit): a host allowlist alone cannot stop
#           exfil to an ATTACKER-OWNED Slack workspace / Telegram bot (both are
#           legitimate slack.com / api.telegram.org FQDNs). Controls landed:
#           (a) Telegram bot-ID path pin (env YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID,
#               inert-until-set) — 516caa66.
#           (b) Slack Web API bot-token pin (env YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN,
#               dual-handle inert-until-set): strips openclaw's Authorization +
#               injects operator's token → forces one workspace.
#           (c) Slack incoming-webhook path pin (env YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_WEBHOOK_PATH,
#               inert-until-set): path encodes workspace+channel+secret → a
#               different attacker-owned webhook has a different path → 403.
#           (d) Egress audit log on every request (including 403 denies) with
#               Authorization header redaction.
#           (e) iptables hashlimit rate-brake on the three egress hosts.
#           CLOSED (v4.1 egress-eval): M4 + secret_detector run on every egress
#           body at gateway /egress/eval/{prefix}/{path} before the deliver hop.
#           OPA mcp_response_decision gates the result; bodies classified RESTRICTED
#           (secrets or injection patterns) are withheld and audited.  Caddy :18790
#           remains the DESTINATION + ACCOUNT control; content control is at gateway.
#           Token/path operator-supplied; never shipped.
#
# Defence in depth: caddy-entrypoint.sh mirrors these three FQDNs in the
# container-level iptables OUTPUT allowlist (YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_EGRESS=1,
# set by install.sh when the openclaw profile is enabled). The Caddyfile
# literals are the PRIMARY control; iptables is belt-and-suspenders.
#
# System-side wiring — unified-sidecar (v4.1 three-agent wrap, 2026-07-07):
# every caller of this listener is a per-system EGRESS FORWARDER
# (egress-openclaw / egress-langflow / egress-letta — codegen §2.3), each
# presenting its system's leaf via tls_client_auth from the SVID mount:
#   https://caddy:18790/llm          (all three systems → gateway:8081 /v1)
#   https://caddy:18790/slack        (openclaw — Slack Web API → slack.com)
#   https://caddy:18790/slack-hooks  (openclaw — response_url → hooks.slack.com)
#   https://caddy:18790/telegram     (openclaw — Bot API → api.telegram.org)
# System-side config uses each system's REAL, schema-validated mechanism:
#   openclaw:  models.providers.yashigani.baseUrl → egress-openclaw:9400/llm/v1
#              channels.telegram.apiRoot          → egress-openclaw:9400/telegram
#              (Slack Web API base URL is NOT configurable in openclaw
#              2026.5.6 — hardcoded slack.com, HTTP CONNECT proxy only; the
#              /slack handles stay for the future proxy/upstream mechanism.
#              The former top-level `egress` config block and the
#              OPENCLAW_*_BASE_URL / OPENCLAW_EGRESS_TLS_* env vars were
#              INVENTED keys the binary rejects/ignores — removed 2026-07-07.)
#   langflow:  OPENAI_API_BASE → http://egress-langflow:9400/llm/v1
#   letta:     OPENAI_API_BASE → http://egress-letta:9400/llm/v1
#
# Static proof (cert chain → SAN gate), per system:
#   ./secrets/<system>_client.crt URI SAN: spiffe://yashigani.internal/<system>
#   (service_identities.yaml) — held by the FORWARDER (sole SVID holder,
#   synthesis #2); stamped-header gates below narrow per prefix
#   (v4.1 LAURA-I1-CEL: CEL URI-SAN expression replaced — caddy 2.11.x *url.URL bug)
# Laura Phase-3 test (c) is live confirmation of this chain.
#
# Port 18790 (front) is distinct from openclaw's own 18789, the Ollama front
# 11435, the mesh listeners 8444/8445, and the codegen MCP mesh-port range
# 9500-9900; never host-mapped (no compose `ports:` entry).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Caller gate — exact SPIFFE SAN match on the VERIFIED peer certificate.
# require_and_verify (below) already guarantees the cert chains to the internal
# intermediate CA; this snippet narrows "any mesh member" to "openclaw only".
#
# v4.1 LAURA-I1-CEL FIX: Caddy 2.11.x cannot evaluate URI SANs in CEL expressions
# (unsupported conversion to ref.Val: (*url.URL)spiffe://...) — the original
# `expression {san.uris.0} != "..."` returned 500 on every request, making the
# gate permanently broken (fail-closed, but openclaw egress completely non-functional).
# Replaced with the proven stamped-header pattern (validated for admin relay):
#   1. request_header bare SET: forge-proof — runs after TLS, overwrites any
#      client-supplied header value before any matcher sees it.
#   2. header matcher (string comparison only) — no CEL, no *url.URL conversion.
#
# Parse-time env substitution: if YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID is unset the
# default literal applies; an empty string would fail CLOSED (no leaf carries an
# empty URI SAN, so the not-header matcher fires and 403s everything).
(openclaw-egress-caller-gate) {
    # Stamp the verified client-cert URI SAN. Bare SET (no + prefix) overwrites
    # any client-supplied value — a caller cannot forge this past the stamp.
    request_header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
    @caller_not_openclaw {
        not header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID:spiffe://yashigani.internal/openclaw}"
    }
    respond @caller_not_openclaw "Forbidden" 403
}

# LLM caller gate — the `llm` egress class (design §2.4, three-agent wrap
# 2026-07-07) is shared by ALL bundled wrapped systems: openclaw, langflow,
# letta. Each per-system egress-forwarder presents its system's leaf to this
# listener; the gate narrows "any mesh member" to exactly the three transitional
# system identities. Same stamped-header pattern as openclaw-egress-caller-gate
# (no CEL — Caddy 2.11.x *url.URL bug). Multiple `not header` lines AND
# together: the matcher fires only when the stamped SPIFFE matches NONE of the
# allowed IDs → 403. Empty env values fail CLOSED (no leaf carries an empty
# URI SAN). This static gate is transitional — caller↔prefix authorisation
# also runs in OPA (egress_grants, closed-world); pin deletion is the
# Laura-gated per-instance migration step (design §3.1 step 2).
(llm-egress-caller-gate) {
    request_header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
    @caller_not_llm_system {
        not header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID:spiffe://yashigani.internal/openclaw}"
        not header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe "{$YASHIGANI_LANGFLOW_SPIFFE_ID:spiffe://yashigani.internal/langflow}"
        not header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe "{$YASHIGANI_LETTA_SPIFFE_ID:spiffe://yashigani.internal/letta}"
    }
    respond @caller_not_llm_system "Forbidden" 403
}

# Deliver caller gate — only the Yashigani gateway mesh peer may call /deliver/*.
# Identical pattern to openclaw-egress-caller-gate (stamped-header, no CEL).
# YASHIGANI_GATEWAY_SPIFFE_ID defaults to spiffe://yashigani.internal/gateway.
# An empty env var fails CLOSED (empty string ≠ any real URI SAN → 403 all).
#
# ORDER MATTERS: Caddy v2.11.x resolves `import <snippet>` at parse time,
# top-to-bottom — a snippet must be DEFINED before the site block that imports
# it ("File to import not found" otherwise).  This definition therefore lives
# ABOVE the :18790 block; the C10 contract test
# (tests/contracts/test_openclaw_egress_single_source.py) parses the file with
# a real caddy binary and catches any regression of this ordering.
(gateway-egress-deliver-gate) {
    request_header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
    @caller_not_gateway {
        not header X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe "{$YASHIGANI_GATEWAY_SPIFFE_ID:spiffe://yashigani.internal/gateway}"
    }
    respond @caller_not_gateway "Forbidden" 403
}

:18790 {
    tls /run/secrets/caddy_client.crt /run/secrets/caddy_client.key {
        client_auth {
            mode require_and_verify
            trust_pool file /run/secrets/ca_intermediate.crt
        }
        protocols tls1.3
        ciphers TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
        curves x25519mlkem768 x25519 secp384r1 secp256r1
    }

    # FP-06 Phase 2 — egress audit log (every outbound request, JSON, stderr).
    # Fires on ALL requests to :18790 — including 403 denies from the caller gate
    # and the bot-ID pin below; denied requests are the most audit-relevant events.
    # Captures: ts, method, URI (upstream-facing after handle_path strip), status,
    # response bytes, duration, TLS client common name (openclaw leaf). The
    # Authorization header (openclaw's bearer tokens to Slack/Telegram) is redacted
    # by the filter encoder — it must NOT appear in cleartext in the log stream.
    log {
        output stderr
        format filter {
            wrap json
            fields {
                request>headers>Authorization delete
            }
        }
    }

    # Outbound body cap — light exfil-volume brake (primary volume/DLP control
    # is FP-06 Phase 2). Matches the public edge cap (OWASP ASVS 4.1.7).
    request_body {
        max_size 10MB
    }

    # ── Phase 1: openclaw → gateway egress evaluation ────────────────────────
    # openclaw dials these paths unchanged (SDK URLs in docker-compose.yml and
    # openclaw.json remain https://caddy:18790/{prefix}/*).  Caddy now:
    #   1. Validates the peer cert (require_and_verify, openclaw SPIFFE stamp).
    #   2. Rewrites the path:  /{prefix}/{...} → /egress/eval/{prefix}/{...}
    #   3. Reverse-proxies the FULL request body to gateway:8080.
    #   4. Sets X-SPIFFE-ID (stamped from verified peer cert URI SAN) and
    #      X-Caddy-Verified-Secret (Layer B HMAC) so the gateway trusts the
    #      caller identity without a second mTLS client-auth handshake.
    # Gateway inspects the body (secret_detector + M4 injection check → OPA)
    # and on ALLOW calls caddy:18790/deliver/{prefix}/{...} (Phase 2 below).
    #
    # Why the stamp is forge-proof: request_header bare SET runs after TLS,
    # before any route handler, overwriting any client-supplied value.
    # X-Caddy-Verified-Secret is read from {$CADDY_INTERNAL_HMAC} at parse
    # time — openclaw has no access to this env var.
    #
    # Transport: (internal-mtls) snippet wires caddy's own client cert +
    # ca_intermediate.crt trust pool for the Caddy → gateway:8080 hop.
    # versions 1.1 matches the min-TLS policy on the gateway listener.

    # ── LLM — wrapped systems /llm/* → gateway /egress/eval/llm/* ──────────
    # Design §2.4 (three-agent wrap 2026-07-07): openclaw + langflow + letta
    # LLM traffic arrives here from each system's egress-forwarder
    # (egress-<system>:9400/llm/* → this listener). Shared prefix, per-system
    # caller identity (the forwarder presents the system leaf). OPA gates the
    # (caller, llm) tuple; the static llm-egress-caller-gate is the
    # transitional belt-and-suspenders. Delivery: /deliver/llm/* below →
    # gateway:8081 (today's inference surface; :8080 openai_router cutover is
    # §2.4 open item (i), Tom).
    handle /llm/* {
        route {
            import llm-egress-caller-gate
            rewrite * /egress/eval{uri}
            reverse_proxy https://gateway:8080 {
                import internal-mtls
                header_up X-SPIFFE-ID {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
                header_up X-Caddy-Verified-Secret {$CADDY_INTERNAL_HMAC}
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Slack Web API — openclaw /slack/* → gateway /egress/eval/slack/* ───
    handle /slack/* {
        route {
            import openclaw-egress-caller-gate
            rewrite * /egress/eval{uri}
            reverse_proxy https://gateway:8080 {
                import internal-mtls
                # Stamp caller SPIFFE (from verified peer cert, already set by
                # openclaw-egress-caller-gate above) onto the gateway-bound request.
                header_up X-SPIFFE-ID {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
                # Layer B HMAC — enables SpiffePeerCertMiddleware Option C to
                # trust the X-SPIFFE-ID without a second mTLS auth handshake.
                header_up X-Caddy-Verified-Secret {$CADDY_INTERNAL_HMAC}
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Slack hooks — openclaw /slack-hooks/* → gateway /egress/eval/slack-hooks/* ──
    handle /slack-hooks/* {
        route {
            import openclaw-egress-caller-gate
            rewrite * /egress/eval{uri}
            reverse_proxy https://gateway:8080 {
                import internal-mtls
                header_up X-SPIFFE-ID {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
                header_up X-Caddy-Verified-Secret {$CADDY_INTERNAL_HMAC}
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Telegram — openclaw /telegram/* → gateway /egress/eval/telegram/* ──
    handle /telegram/* {
        route {
            import openclaw-egress-caller-gate
            rewrite * /egress/eval{uri}
            reverse_proxy https://gateway:8080 {
                import internal-mtls
                header_up X-SPIFFE-ID {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0}
                header_up X-Caddy-Verified-Secret {$CADDY_INTERNAL_HMAC}
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Phase 2: gateway → final delivery ───────────────────────────────────
    # These routes are ONLY reachable by the Yashigani gateway mesh peer.
    # The gateway calls them after egress inspection + OPA ALLOW.
    # All FP-06 Phase 2 destination/account controls (bot-token workspace pin,
    # webhook path pin, bot-ID pin) live here — they are enforced AFTER the
    # content gate, in the same Caddy block as before.
    #
    # Caller gate: gateway-egress-deliver-gate (snippet defined below) stamps
    # X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe from the peer cert and checks against
    # YASHIGANI_GATEWAY_SPIFFE_ID.  Only the gateway cert satisfies this check.
    # openclaw cannot call these routes — its SPIFFE ≠ gateway SPIFFE.

    # ── Deliver: Slack Web API — https://slack.com/api/... ──────────────────
    handle_path /deliver/slack/* {
        route {
            import gateway-egress-deliver-gate
            # FP-06 Phase 2 — Slack bot-token workspace pin.
            # Inert-when-empty: same semantics as the original /slack/* block.
            @slack_pin_on expression "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN:}" != ""
            handle @slack_pin_on {
                reverse_proxy https://slack.com {
                    header_up -Authorization
                    header_up Authorization "Bearer {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN:}"
                    header_up Host slack.com
                    header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                    header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                    header_up -X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe
                    transport http {
                        tls
                        tls_server_name slack.com
                    }
                }
            }
            handle {
                reverse_proxy https://slack.com {
                    header_up Host slack.com
                    header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                    header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                    header_up -X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe
                    transport http {
                        tls
                        tls_server_name slack.com
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Deliver: Slack hooks — https://hooks.slack.com/... ──────────────────
    handle_path /deliver/slack-hooks/* {
        route {
            import gateway-egress-deliver-gate
            # FP-06 Phase 2 — Slack incoming-webhook path pin.
            # Inert-when-empty: same semantics as the original /slack-hooks/* block.
            @wrong_slack_hook expression "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_WEBHOOK_PATH:}" != "" && !{http.request.uri.path}.startsWith("{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_WEBHOOK_PATH:}")
            respond @wrong_slack_hook "Forbidden" 403
            reverse_proxy https://hooks.slack.com {
                header_up Host hooks.slack.com
                header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                header_up -X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe
                transport http {
                    tls
                    tls_server_name hooks.slack.com
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Deliver: Telegram Bot API — https://api.telegram.org/... ────────────
    handle_path /deliver/telegram/* {
        route {
            import gateway-egress-deliver-gate
            # FP-06 Phase 2 — Telegram bot-ID pin.
            # Inert-when-empty: same semantics as the original /telegram/* block.
            @wrong_bot_id expression "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID:}" != "" && !{http.request.uri.path}.startsWith("/bot{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID:}:")
            respond @wrong_bot_id "Forbidden" 403
            reverse_proxy https://api.telegram.org {
                header_up Host api.telegram.org
                header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                header_up -X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe
                transport http {
                    tls
                    tls_server_name api.telegram.org
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Deliver: LLM — gateway inference surface (INTERNAL class) ──────────
    # deliver_to `gateway-inference` (codegen reserved internal class): the
    # ONLY deliver handle that never leaves the mesh. Target is gateway:8081
    # — the same OpenAI-compatible surface the agents dialled directly before
    # the wrap (plain HTTP; AgentAuthMiddleware bearer auth still applies at
    # the app layer — the agents' Authorization headers traverse eval →
    # deliver untouched). handle_path strips /deliver/llm so the upstream
    # sees /v1/... exactly as before. §2.4 open item (i): cutover to the
    # mTLS :8080 openai_router is Tom's follow-up; until confirmed, :8081
    # preserves today's working semantics with governance added in front.
    handle_path /deliver/llm/* {
        route {
            import gateway-egress-deliver-gate
            reverse_proxy http://gateway:8081 {
                header_up Host gateway
                header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                header_up -X-Yashigani-Verified-Spiffe
            }
        }
    }

    # Default-deny: no other destination exists on this listener.
    handle {
        respond "Forbidden" 403
    }
}
